deep.reconstruction:
Context (pp. 14-16). L is a totally ordered set with two standing hypotheses. It is 'filtrant' both ways, meaning it has no endpoints. It is 'divisible', meaning dense: for x<y there is z with x<z<y. These are the conditions later used to define 'Thomson ordonnés' on p.16.

The data are:
- the 'découpage' families Drop_2(L) and Drop_3(L);
- for each pair ε ∈ Drop_2(L), the set C(ε) = {t ∈ L−ε : ε∪{t} ∈ Drop_3(L)};
- the connected components of C(ε).

The aim, announced on p.14, is to show that these data determine the strict betweenness relation R(a,b,c), 'b strictly between a and c'. Together with Drop_2, that relation reconstructs L up to reversing the order.

Argument on this page. Take a triple J={a,b,c} ∈ Drop_3(L) and suppose a<b<c. For each 2-subset of J, form the component of the complement-data containing the remaining point:
- X_{a,b} = component of C({a,b}) containing c = L_{>b};
- X_{b,c} = component of C({b,c}) containing a = L_{<b};
- X_{c,a} = component of C({c,a}) containing b = ]a,c[.

The intersections are:
- X_{a,b} ∩ X_{b,c} = ∅;
- X_{a,b} ∩ X_{c,a} = ]b,c[, nonempty by density;
- X_{b,c} ∩ X_{c,a} = ]a,b[, nonempty by density.

So the middle point is intrinsically characterized: it is the unique element of J such that, for the two pairs ε, ε' ∈ P_2(J) containing it, X_ε ∩ X_ε' = ∅. Hence R(a,b,c) is recoverable from the data ('On gagne !'). The NB stresses that density was used again.

The next page abstracts the needed hypotheses into a maximal full subcategory of ordered sets ('Thomson' ordered sets). It then raises an open question: axiomatize which relations R(a,b,c), or which data Soc P_2(L) with R_ε, come from such structures.
